{
  "requirement": "Each public SDK item has one defining module path. The SDK exposes no public\nre-exports. The chapter that introduces each supported type or free function owns its declaration.\nThe [public API inventory](sdk/public-api.md) links each item to that requirement,\nwhich describes its purpose\nand declares its public Rust interface, including fields, enum variants, trait\nmembers, and inherent methods. A change to that interface requires a change to\nthe owning requirement. Every supported struct with public fields is\n`#[non_exhaustive]`. Its constructor accepts the values needed to create it.\nAdditional fields have consuming builder methods that accept a concrete value.\nOptional fields start as `None`. Backend inference options start empty and can\nbe added one key at a time.\n\nThe SDK’s public modules follow the interfaces introduced in these chapters.\n\n- `domain`: application data and the `Domain` and `NodeContents` traits.\n- `graph`: criterion definitions, graph observations, evidence, and functions for running criteria.\n- `request`: `ValidationContext`, typed `Ask` builders, context values, and request phases.\n- `agent`: configuration for the supplied agents.\n- `sources`: project inspection, search queries, and their results.\n- `outline`: selectable source contents and language rules; `outline::languages`\n  supplies bundled languages, and `outline::selector` defines selector syntax.\n- `storage`: filesystem evidence storage.\n\nSource access, search engines, redaction, provider transports, agent-result paging,\nand conversation machinery are implementation details of those interfaces.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.public_api`. I found no unacknowledged gap.\n\n**Single defining path and no public re-exports**\n- `tests/public_api.rs#fn[sdk_has_no_public_reexports]` parses every `.rs` file under `src` with tree-sitter. It fails on any `use_declaration` with a `pub` visibility modifier. A leftover `pub use` would therefore be caught.\n- `tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` generates rustdoc and walks every module's item tables. It asserts that a path/kind set exactly equals the inventory in `md/requirements/sdk/public-api.md`. It also rejects duplicate rustdoc exports. This covers the listed modules and the rule that source access, search engines and similar machinery are not exported, because any extra public item shows up as \"Unexpected\".\n\n**Inventory links each item to its owning requirement, and the interface is declared there**\n- `specified_exports`, `declaration_target` and `check_declaration` (called from `documented_exports`) require a declaration link for every non-module item. They load the linked `:::{req}` block and require prose plus a Rust listing.\n- `check_declaration` compares the following against rustdoc:\n  - field and method names;\n  - trait implementations and associated types;\n  - the item declaration, covering fields, variants and trait members;\n  - inherent method signatures.\n- A change to the public interface without a change to the owning requirement therefore fails the test.\n- `tests/public_api.rs#fn[member_order_is_free_but_field_and_method_types_are_committed]` checks the comparison helper itself. Reordering methods is accepted. Changing a field type, a return type or a `&mut self`/`&self` receiver is rejected.\n\n**`#[non_exhaustive]` on every struct with public fields**\n- `check_declaration` asserts that any struct page with `structfield.` anchors has `#[non_exhaustive]` in its published declaration. It runs for every exported struct.\n\n**Constructors, consuming builders and concrete values**\n- The signature comparison pins constructor parameters, `self`-by-value builder receivers and concrete parameter types against the requirement listings.\n- Behavioural tests exercise the convention on representative structs from each relevant module:\n  - `agent/config.rs#mod[tests]/fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]` covers `BedrockConfig`, `OpenAiConfig`, `OllamaConfig` and `BedrockCredentials`. It checks that `new()` sets only the required values, with optional fields absent and `options` empty. Builders chain by value. `option()` adds one key at a time, and a repeated key replaces the earlier value.\n  - `outline_content.rs#fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` covers `OutlineItem` and `Outline`. Optional spans start as `None`, and builders set them.\n  - `selector.rs#mod[tests]/fn[parses_named_anonymous_and_ordinal_segments]` covers `Segment::new` and `ordinal()`, and `SelectorError::new`.\n  - `source_defaults.rs` covers `SourceFile` (`language` starts `None`, the `language()` builder), `TextQuery` and `StructuralQuery` (`limit` starts `None`, `scope()`, `limit()` and `context_lines()` builders), and `LanguageId`.\n- The remaining structs follow the same convention. Their signatures are pinned by the exports test, and their behaviour is covered by the tests for their owning requirements.\n\n**Cited and delegated obligations**\n- Per-item behaviour, such as search semantics, is delegated to the owning requirements' tests. It is not reassessed here.\n\nTogether, the static rustdoc-versus-requirement comparison and the sampled behavioural tests cover each obligation in the text.",
  "references": "83e5a024def69d2c249a2488b0d6e064785b414d91c24616a0f007d173d67065",
